bility 3? Solves implied problem related to stated problem: do X to get implied Y which applies. The logging mandate specifically requires logs of events relevant for risk identification, post-market monitoring, etc., and for Annex III point1(a) specific items. Provenance pipeline does not record period of each use, reference database against which input checked, input data for match, identification of natural persons verification. So it may be some benefit within special situation.\n\nThe anchored: 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). The stated problem is meet logging mandate. Does this pipeline meet logging mandate? It records execution logs in real-time and snapshots on update. Might partially meet automatic recording of events over lifetime. Recital 71 requires automatic recording of events by means of logs over duration of lifetime. Pipeline links execution logs. Could be direct.\n\nMaybe applicability 3: solves implied problem related to stated problem (traceability/compliance). I'd say applicability = 3? Or 4?\n\nLet's think: Article12(1) shall technically allow for automatic recording of events logs over lifetime. Proposal triggers automatic snapshots whenever high-risk system is updated, and links execution logs in real-time. That addresses logging. Also Recital71 requires technical documentation containing information necessary to assess compliance... including general characteristics, algorithms, data, training, testing validation processes... Pipeline maps model iteration to training data and hyperparameter set – that is exactly technical documentation.\n\nSo it directly relates. I'd give applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute\nDoes pipeline solve logging mandate? It provides provenance but does not explicitly record period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. For general high-risk systems, maybe sufficient? Article12(2) requires recording events relevant for identifying situations risk, facilitating post-market monitoring, monitoring operation. Pipeline maps iterations to training data – may contribute but not guarantee logs of usage events.\n\nProbably effectiveness 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving\n\nProposal transforms regulatory burden into powerful tool for scientific reproducibility. That's changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations: logging woven into science. That sounds paradigm stretching = 3? Or paradigm breaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Might be paradigm breaking? It addresses larger problem of what lab researches? It says transforms burden into tool for reproducibility, which is larger problem? Maybe not change what they research, just how.\n\nParadigm_relatedness probably 3 (paradigm stretching: changes that relationship). Could be 2? Introduces metadata layer graph databases – new tools, still treats compliance as logging practice? But they claim transforms burden into tool. I think 3.\n\nOriginality: 4 not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 interesting 1 common mundane\n\nAutomated provenance pipeline with graph database linking datasets code commits execution logs is fairly common in MLops. Might be interesting but not rare. Could be 2 = interesting