teresting? Standardized software library to automate event recording is common practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Centralized logging middleware is a common engineering solution widely used for regulatory and operational traceability.\n\nScore 1.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nThis is acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Automating logs via internal software does not violate legal, social or political norms and aligns with Article 12 requirements.\n\nScore 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDevelop API-driven middleware hooks into training loops and inference endpoints across all projects. That's significant change, engineering effort. Might be some changes reasonably feasible? Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Developing a unified library for all pipelines is non-trivial but feasible. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Building and integrating an API middleware across diverse training and inference pipelines requires engineering effort but is reasonably feasible with dedicated team resources.\n\nScore 3.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses how to meet logging mandate. Do X (unified logging layer) to get Y (automatic event recording and metadata capture). That's directly related. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: The middleware directly automates event recording and metadata capture required by Article 12 for high-risk AI syst