ive event triggering solve logging mandate? It would contribute to meeting mandate but is research; if successful it would help satisfy Article12. Might be partial? It aims to satisfy Article12 without straining infrastructure. If you could do it, it would solve main problem of compliance cost. Reasonable. Could be 4? Maybe 3 because it's only a partial solution? The logging mandate requires recording events relevant for... Developing methods may not guarantee compliance with all requirements like specific data points in Art12(3). But high-fidelity logging methods could satisfy.\n\nProbably effectiveness = 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but it is only partial solution). Research initiative is not guaranteed. Hmm \"if you could do it, it would solve the main problem\". If research succeeds, would it solve? Might still need implementation. I'd say 3: reasonable and will contribute.\n\nLet's think anchored: 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability. The stated problem is decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Developing methods is a way to meet mandate. Could be full solution.\n\nBut note approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So effectiveness should consider that it's a research project, not immediate implementation. The problem demands decision now. Research initiative may not immediately meet mandate. So effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes but partial.\n\nI'll go with effectiveness 3.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is scientific project focused on developing high-fidelity logging methods... aligns regulatory necessity with scientific ambition by optimizing efficiency. That's weaving logging into science? It changes relationship? Maybe slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (compressed formats, selective triggering) but still treats compliance as logging practice. It doesn't change what lab researches fundamentally? It does align regulatory necessity with scientific ambition.\n\nProbably paradigm_relatedness = 2 or 3. \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science)\" That matches description of 3. It says aligns regulatory necessity with scientific ambition. So changes relationship. Might be 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination 2 = interesting 1 = common mundane boring\n\nLow-overhead traceability research initiative with compressed logging formats and selective event triggering. That's somewhat common in engineering. Not particularly ingenious. Probably originality 2 = interesting. Could be 3? Unusual? Hmm experienced evaluator would expect labs to propose optimization of logging. Might be common.\n\nI'll rate originality 2 (interesting).\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 easy low cost non-radical changes. 3 some changes reasonably feasible. 2 significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible. 1 totally infeasible.\n\nProposal is a scientific project focused on developing... requires research effort, scarce implementation resources. It's not easy; it's a research initiative requiring time and staff. Some changes, reasonably feasible? Developing compressed logging formats may be significant change. Might be 2 = significant change expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. Since approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. Research is exploring whether idea might work. So implementability low.\n\nImplementability = 2? Could be 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\". Research project is more than some changes. Might be significant change.\n\nI'll go with 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. 4 common strategies violate no norms. 3 somewhat uncommon but do not offend. 2 offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable. 1 radically violates laws...\n\nResearch to comply with regulation is acceptable. So 4.\n\nNow produce output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability.\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nProbably format:\n\nP2252\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure evidence sentence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
