committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding explore.\n\nAutomated audit tooling verifies logs contain fields. Does it meet logging mandate? It checks compliance but doesn't create logs. It helps ensure compliance but does not create logging capability. Might contribute partially. Effectiveness? It will contribute to solution - provides go-no-go signal. But does not actually produce logs. The mandate requires technically allow automatic recording. Tool just audits. So it's partial solution. Possibly 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps, but only partial). Could be 2 = unlikely to solve problem because you still need logging. Hmm.\n\nThe problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Auditing logs assumes logs exist. It doesn't help create them. But it helps ensure compliance. Might be partial. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt applies to stated problem: checking logs for Article 12 fields is directly related. So 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Yes. Might be 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is audit tooling, acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nValidation scripts scanning logs is easy. Likely 4 = easy to implement at low cost.\n\nNow produce output with id and each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating echoing its id exactly. Provide lines.\n\