ols, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\". SDK is a new tool. Might be slightly paradigm stretching. I'd lean to 2? Let's see instrument. Probably they want paradigm preserving because it's just building wrapper for logs. It's standard. I'll go with 1? Might be 2.\n\nLet's choose 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Evidence: SDK adds a shared tool but keeps compliance as separate logging task.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping shared library wrapper around existing telemetry tools across diverse model architectures is some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 3. Not easy low cost? Requires development effort. Could be 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nYes, SDK for compliance is acceptable. So 4.\n\nNow format: Return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nOrder listed: applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide maybe JSON? Probably list.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid P1162\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The SDK directly provides automatic recording of Article 12 event types to meet the logging mandate facing the lab. -> score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, the standardized API and middleware would capture required timestamps, references and session IDs across models, solving the core traceability requirement. -> score 4\n\noriginality_evidence: Building a shared logging SDK wrapped around existing telemetry is a common engineering response to regulatory record-keeping. -> score 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a new tool but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice without changing how R&D is conducted. -> score 2? Wait if we chose 1, sentence should match. Let's decide final.\n\nI think paradigm_relatedness = 1 (paradigm preserving). Sentence: The proposal treats compliance as standard logging practice and leaves the lab’s R&D workflow unchanged. Score 1.\n\nImplementability_evidence: Wrapping existing telemetry into a shared library requires moderate engineering effort across diverse architectures but is reasonably feasible. -> score 3\n\nAcceptability_evidence: A unified compliance SDK is a common, legally compliant strategy that offends no norms or sensibilities. -> score 4\n\nReturn.\n\nEnsure format clear.\n\n",
