ing; shows some imagination\n2 interesting\n1 common, mundane, boring\n\nShared software library for logging is common, mundane. Probably 1 or 2? Common strategies. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nRelevance applicability (1-4):\n4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 solves implied problem...\nIdea directly addresses logging mandate: automatic standardized event logging for high-risk AI deployments captures timestamps. Directly applies. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\nWill unified framework solve logging mandate? It ensures consistent regulatory compliance. Seems would solve. Possibly 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProvide output echoing id exactly. Format? Probably JSON? Instruction: give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Might output lines.\n\nProbably produce JSON with fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2430\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure order listed.\n\nEnsure one sentence each evidence.\n\nProceed.",
