tial. Could be 4? If you could do it, would it solve main problem? Main problem is meet logging mandate. Dashboard alone does not ensure logs are automatically recorded per Art12(1). It analyzes logs. So maybe not full solve. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability: socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. 4 = common strategies... Monitoring dashboard is common. Score 4.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented. Some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Sidecar agent analyzing logs for predefined triggers + dashboard. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Could be moderate. Might be 3. Could be 4? Easy to implement at low cost? Probably requires engineering effort. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. Levels: 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance.\n\nProposal is dashboard for logs; still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new element tool? It's a monitoring agent. Probably slightly paradigm stretching? It transforms raw logs into actionable alerts, maybe just tool. Might be paradigm preserving? Standard compliance practice: monitoring logs. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Score 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare ingenious... Common mundane boring? Real-time risk dashboard is fairly common in MLOps. Probably common, mundane. Level 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 interesting? Might be 2 interesting. Hmm. Reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Dashboard for logs is expected. So originality 1.\n\nLet's produce output with id P2464 and fields.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Not specified. Probably just lines. Safer to output structured.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2464\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nLet's do that.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure order: applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality.\n\nProceed.",
