egree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nTraceability validation sandbox is interesting but maybe common? CI/CD testing for logging is fairly standard practice. Could be 2 = interesting. Might be 3 unusual? I think it's somewhat common in engineering. I'd say 2.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps, partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources... This sandbox catches gaps early preventing costly redesigns. It helps ensure compliance but does not itself implement logging. It is a validation gate. Will contribute to solution? Probably contributes but doesn't fully meet logging mandate; it ensures logs are complete. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial). Maybe 4? It will solve stated problem without regard workability? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. A validation sandbox helps meet mandate but does not create logs. Might be partial. I think 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\nProposal does X to get sufficient traceability for post-market monitoring. That's directly related. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instructions: Return rating... For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\"...\n\nMight output as lines.\n\nI'll produce structured output:\n\nid: P2049\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a CI/CD sandbox with automated edge-case tests requires engineering effort but uses existing DevOps tooling, making it reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Testing logging completeness is a standard quality assurance practice that does not violate legal or social norms. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The sandbox adds new testing tools and processes around compliance but still treats logging as a separate record-keeping requirement rather than integrating it into research. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: While validation environments are common in software engineering, applying them specifically to EU AI Act log completeness is an interesting but not surprising adaptation. Score 2? Might be 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination. Hmm. Could be 2 = interesting. I'll pick 2.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The sandbox can catch missing logs before release and reduce redesign costs, which helps meet Article 12 but does not itself create the required logging capabilities. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets ensuring automatic event recording and traceability for post-market monitoring as mandated by Article 12. Score 4.\n\nMake sure each evidence is one sentence.\n\nProvide output.\n\nProbably JSON format. I'll output key-value.\n\nLet's produce final.",
