        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P2881\",\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Internal pre-deployment compliance audits are a common, legally expected quality gate that violates no norms or sensibilities.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 4,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Creating a formal audit process with simulation tests requires hiring/training compliance officers and adding release gates, which is reasonably feasible but involves some organizational changes.\",\n      \"implementability\": 3,\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"The protocol verifies existing logging capabilities but does not itself build the automatic logging required by Article 12, so it contributes to compliance without solving the mandate alone.\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 3,\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"Verifying record-keeping before release directly addresses the lab's need to meet Article 12 logging requirements and avoid liability.\",\n      \"applicability\": 4,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Pre-deployment regulatory audits are a standard, mundane industry practice for compliance.\",\n      \"originality\": 1,\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"The proposal treats compliance as a separate final gate and leaves the lab's R&D workflow unchanged.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 1\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need output rating for proposal P2881.\n\nProposal: Pre-Deployment Regulatory Audit Protocol. Formal internal certification process to verify system's record-keeping capabilities meet EU AI Act standards. Acts as final quality gate before public release. Compliance officers conduct simulation tests where trigger risk events and verify logs capture necessary data points. Systems must pass audit to receive release signature. Prevents non-compliant systems from entering market, directly protecting from legal liability.\n\nWe need six subscales in order: acceptability, implementability, effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nEach with one sentence evidence then score.\n\nWorkability acceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws or sensibilities...\n\nEvidence: Internal audit for compliance is common, non-offensive. So acceptability 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nAudit protocol requires compliance officers, simulation tests. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3? Could be easy? Need formal internal certification process, quality gate. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. I'd say 3. Could be 4? Might need resources. I think 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Accept.\n\nRelevance effectiveness (1-4): degree idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes audit protocol solve logging mandate? It verifies record-keeping capabilities meet standards, prevents non-compliant systems from entering market. It doesn't actually build logging capabilities, just checks them. So it contributes to compliance but does not create logs. Effectiveness maybe 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps ensure compliance). But problem is how to meet logging mandate. Audit alone doesn't meet mandate; need logging. Might be partial solution. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nAudit applies directly to meeting logging mandate via verification. It's directly related. Could be 4? The stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Audit is about verifying compliance, which is directly related. Maybe 4. Or 3? I think applicability 4.\n\nNovelty originality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nPre-deployment audit protocol is common practice. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nAudit is standard compliance practice. Paradigm preserving. So 1.\n\nCheck order: acceptability_evidence then acceptability, implementability_evidence then implementability, effectiveness_evidence then effectiveness, applicability_evidence then applicability, originality_evidence then originality, paradigm_relatedness_evidence then paradigm_relatedness.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably output lines. Might be plain text. Safer to give structured output with id and fields.\n\nThe instruction: Return the rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\"...\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P2881\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nLet's do that.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Internal pre-deployment compliance audits are a common, legally expected quality gate that violates no norms or sensibilities. -> score 4\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating a formal audit process with simulation tests requires hiring/training compliance officers and adding release gates, which is reasonably feasible but involves some organizational changes. -> score 3\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The protocol verifies existing logging capabilities but does not itself build the automatic logging required by Article 12, so it contributes to compliance without solving the mandate alone. -> score 3\n\nApplicability evidence: Verifying record-keeping before release directly addresses the lab's need to meet Article 12 logging requirements and avoid liability. -> score 4\n\nOriginality evidence: Pre-deployment regulatory audits are a standard, mundane industry practice for compliance. -> score 1\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal treats compliance as a separate final gate and leaves the lab's R&D workflow unchanged. -> score 1\n\nCheck definitions: paradigm preserving = standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Yes.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
