art of stated problem). Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Generating compliance reports is not meeting logging mandate itself, but it's about documentation required by regulation. Recital 71 says technical documentation needed. So maybe directly related.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? If you could do it, would solve main problem? It reduces admin burden but does not ensure logs are captured per Article 12(2)(3). Might be partial.\n\nProbably effectiveness = 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial).\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving standard compliance.\n\nAutomated pipeline is a tool/process for compliance documentation. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements tools? Probably slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious. Automated compliance doc pipeline via CI/CD trigger generating reports. That's fairly common DevOps practice. Probably common, mundane, boring =1. Could be interesting? Maybe unusual? I think common. So originality 1.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. CI/CD trigger to generate reports from logs. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Likely 3. Not easy low cost? Might be some changes. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAcceptability