        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Need rating.\n\nProposal P1298: Universal Logging Framework Implementation. centralized software library providing standardized API calls for event recording and precise timestamping across all high-risk AI models... modular middleware captures input data, output results, internal system states automatically without disrupting core model logic. Integrate into CI/CD pipeline.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is centralized library for logging. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching? It doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations fundamentally, just makes logging easier. Probably 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3= unusual interesting shows some imagination 2=interesting 1=common mundane boring\n\nCentralized logging framework for AI compliance is fairly common, mundane. Might be interesting but common. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 interesting? Probably common practice in software engineering. So 1.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProblem: must decide how to respond: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nUniversal logging framework would provide automatic recording of events, traceability, etc. Seems reasonable and will solve main problem? It provides logs but Article 12 also requires specific logging for point 1(a) Annex III systems: period of each use, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons... Framework captures input data output results internal system states automatically. Could meet mandate. Might be partial? Probably 4 = will solve stated problem without regard workability.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\nYes do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not totally impossible\nDevelop modular middleware, integrate into CI/CD across all high-risk models. That's significant change, requires engineering team development. Might be 3? Some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost. Probably 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nYes logging framework is acceptable. 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProvide list.\n\nProbably JSON? Instructions say return rating echoing its id exactly. Provide each subscale evidence then score.\n\nWill produce structured output.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft:\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal introduces a new logging tool and process but keeps compliance as separate logging practice rather than changing R&D-compliance relationship.\nScore 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Centralized middleware logging libraries for regulatory traceability are