olve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal addresses Article 12(3)(d) identification of natural persons involved in verification. But mandate is broader: automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of persons. Proposal only covers identification of persons for verification. It does not cover logging period, reference DB, input data etc. So partial solution.\n\nEffectiveness = 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution but only partial. Yes.\n\nEvidence: It ensures traceability of human verification as required but does not address other logging elements like usage timestamps or input data.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\nProposal directly addresses Article 12(3)(d) requirement. That's directly related to stated problem of meeting logging mandate. So applicability 4? It solves identified problem directly related to stated problem. Yes.\n\nEvidence: The mandate explicitly requires identifying natural persons verifying results, which the protocol targets.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare ingenious imaginative surprising.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nHuman-in-the-loop accountability with digital signatures for high-risk AI verification. That's fairly common compliance practice. Probably common, mundane. Might be 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nEvidence: Identity management and digital signatures for audit trails are standard industry practices for regulated systems.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: