mon. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Evidence: standardized plug-and-play logging wrappers are a conventional software engineering solution.\n\neffectiveness (1-4)\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution...\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? Captures events timestamps required by Article12. Probably will solve. It automates capture, ensures consistent compliance. So 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Could be 4.\n\nEvidence: Automatic capture of events/timestamps via wrappers directly meets Article12 logging requirement.\n\napplicability (1-4)\n4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nDirectly addresses how to meet logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Library provides the automatic event recording mandated for high-risk prototypes.\n\nacceptability (1-4)\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nSecure immutable ledger is acceptable, no violation. Common strategies. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Using wrappers and immutable logs aligns with legal traceability norms and does not offend sensibilities.\n\nimplementability (1-4)\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nImplementing unified library with wrappers around core loops is some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be significant? Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Building wrappers and a shared ledger requires engineering effort across prototypes but is feasible within an R&D lab.\n\nReturn rat