gging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal weaves logging into scientific reproducibility; it integrates compliance into research workflow. That's changing relationship? It says ensures perfect scientific reproducibility while simultaneously satisfying... That suggests logging woven into science. That is paradigm stretching? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. The proposal doesn't change what lab researches, just adds ledger. Could be 2 or 3.\n\nDefinition: 3 = changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). It says logging woven into science. That matches example. So paradigm_relatedness 3.\n\nEvidence: By embedding mandatory logs inside an experiment versioning system for reproducibility, compliance becomes part of scientific workflow rather than separate admin task.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 easy to implement low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 significant change, or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nImplementing integrated versioning system with containerized environments hashing all inputs/outputs automatically is significant engineering effort, requires infrastructure changes. Not trivial. Some labs have this, but implementing from scratch is significant. Might be 2 = significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible. Could be 3 reasonably feasible. The proposal says automated ledger using containerized environments... That is substantial. I'd say 2.\n\nEvidence: Building an immutable, container-based hashing ledger across all experiments demands substantial engineering, data storage, and workflow changes.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 radically violates laws/