ls: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nI think it's partial contribution: helps traceability, reference database, but doesn't directly provide logs of each use period, input data matches, etc. So effectiveness =3? Could be 4? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Lineage registry addresses part of mandate (reference database). Might not solve full logging mandate. Probably contribute.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\nProposal does X to get verifiable chain of custody for system state, ensures technical viability of reference database requirement. That is directly related to Article 12(3)(b). So applicability =4? It solves identified problem directly related. Yes.\n\nWorkability:\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nImmutable hashing lineage is common best practice, does not offend. Acceptable. Probably 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive...\nIntegrating into CI/CD, metadata store, cryptographic hashing versioning every dataset... That's significant change, expensive? Could be reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost. So 3.\n\nNovelty:\noriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious...\nImmutable Dataset and Model Lineage Registry is common MLOps practice, not rare nor ingenious. Could be common mundane boring =>1? Or interesting =2. Might be common. I think originality =2? Let's interpret: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3= unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2= interesting. 1= common mundane boring.\n\nThis is fairly standard lineage tracking. Maybe common mundane. I'd say 1 or 2. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Many labs do model registry. So 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis proposal introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's slightly paradigm stretching? It integrates into CI/CD, links model versions to database snapshots. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nAlternatively could be paradigm preserving =1 because standard compliance. Hmm.\n\nThe description: \"introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\" =>2.\n\nThus ratings:\neffectiveness_evidence: ... score 3\napplicability_evidence... score 4\nacceptability_evidence... score 4\nimplementability_evidence... score 3\noriginality_evidence... score 1 (or 2). Let's pick 1.\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence... score 2\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating before number.\n\nProvide output JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably format as JSON object with id and fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\n{\n \"id\": \"P1896\",\n \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"...\",\n \"effectiveness\": 3,\n ...\n}\n\nEnsure order listed.\n\nMake sentences concise.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The registry creates verifiable lineage for datasets and models but does not automatically record per-use periods, matched inputs, or verifier identities required by Article 12(1)-(3).\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly targets the Article 12(3)(b) reference-database requirement and traceability needed for high-risk AI logging.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Cryptographic hashing and versioning are standard engineering practices that violate no legal or social norms.\n\nImplementability evidence: Integrating a metadata store and hashing into CI/CD requires non-trivial engineering changes and ongoing maintenance beyond a low-cost tweak.\n\nOriginality evidence: Immutable dataset and model lineage registries are common MLOps tooling and are mundane rather than ingenious.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds new tooling for logging traceability but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than reshaping R&D or compliance relationship.\n\nReturn.\n\nLet's output.",
